{
  "readme": "Grams per household unit per canonical item, for qty->grams conversion (R100). Weight units are universal (lb=453.592, oz=28.3495, kg=1000, g=1). Volume/each units below. Precision matters for macro-dense items; near-zero-macro items (spices, herbs, vinegars) use coarse standards - gram error there is macro- and cost-irrelevant. 'can' = default whole-can grams when no size given (parser prefers explicit parenthetical sizes). cooked_to_dry: factor applied when source says 'cooked' for rice/pasta (we list dry).",
  "defaults": { "sauce_tbsp": 16, "sauce_tsp": 5.3, "sauce_cup": 250, "spice_tsp": 2, "spice_tbsp": 6, "herb_cup": 16, "floz": 29.57 },
  "cooked_to_dry": { "Rice": 0.36, "Penne Pasta": 0.44, "Spaghetti": 0.44, "Ziti Pasta": 0.44, "Rotini Pasta": 0.44, "Egg Noodles": 0.44, "Orzo Pasta": 0.44 },
  "basis_reconciliation_2026_08_07": "THIS FILE AND food-macros-db.json BOTH STATE WHAT A CUP WEIGHS, and until 2026-08-07 nobody compared them. 13 items disagreed. Where food-macros-db carries a TRANSCRIBED PRODUCT LABEL and this file carried a generic figure, the label wins and this file moved: Salsa cup 260->240 + tbsp 16->15 (label 2 tbsp = 30 g), Salsa Verde cup 260->240 (+tbsp 15 added), Coconut Milk cup 226->240 (label 1/3 cup = 80 g), Teriyaki Sauce tbsp 16->18 (label 1 tbsp = 18 g; the cup already agreed), Parmesan Cheese cup 90->112 + tbsp 5.6->7 (label 1/4 cup = 28 g). DELIBERATELY NOT RECONCILED: the canned-bean and corn cups (172/165 here vs 260/250 implied by the labels) are DRAINED yields against AS-PACKED label servings - two different measurements of two different things, and merging them would be the error, not the fix. Fresh Basil (cup 24 whole leaves vs 2.5 g/tbsp chopped) and Green Onions (cup 50 vs 6 g/tbsp) are the same story in produce. Rice was the sixth and is settled - see rice_basis below.",
  "rice_basis_2026_08_07": "RICE IS 180 g/cup: 1/4 cup dry = 45 g, which is what Member's Mark Long Grain White Rice and Mahatma Extra Long Grain both state on the bag. It was 185 (USDA's figure for the same food) while food-macros-db.json's Rice row was a transcribed Member's Mark THAI JASMINE label at 1/4 cup = 50 g -> 200 g/cup. Two different rices, and the COST math had already picked one: the board's 'White Rice' commodity is long grain at all seven stores (Member's Mark Long Grain 50 lb, Doguet's, Mahatma, Kroger/Hy-Vee/Pampa store brands) - not one jasmine capture among them. So the jasmine row was the odd one out, and BOTH files now sit on the long-grain bag's own label. Brad's call, 2026-08-07. The food-macros-db Rice row moved with it (45 g / 160 cal / 3 P / 36 C); per-gram that is a 1.2% calorie change, and the seven recipes that hand-label their rice 'cups dry jasmine' were left alone because a writer naming a specific rice is a claim about that recipe, not about this table. friendly-amt-lib.ps1 READS this value now instead of hardcoding it in four places, so changing it here changes it everywhere.",
  "spice_densities_2026_08_28": [
    "Caraway Seeds and Poultry Seasoning had NO density entry of any kind, so friendly-amt-lib fell all",
    "the way through to its weight fallback and two live cards asked for '0.11 oz' of a spice - 3 g, which",
    "no home scale resolves. UNMEASURABLE-QTY flagged both and repair-unmeasurable-qty could not fix",
    "either, because there was nothing to convert to.",
    "",
    "The figures are coarse by design and this file's readme says so: near-zero-macro items (spices,",
    "herbs, vinegars) use coarse standards because gram error there is macro- and cost-irrelevant.",
    "defaults.spice_tsp = 2 is the estate's own declared figure for this case. Both entries are placed",
    "with their FAMILY rather than on that flat default: Caraway Seeds is a whole seed and takes 2.1,",
    "the same as Ground Cumin and Ground Cloves beside it. Poultry Seasoning is a ground LEAF blend",
    "(sage, thyme, rosemary, marjoram) and takes 1.5, between the dried leaf herbs at 1 and the ground",
    "spices at 2.",
    "",
    "SECOND EFFECT, deliberate: pipeline\\parse-compute.ps1 falls back to defaults.sauce_tsp (5.3) when an",
    "item has no tsp, so a future source line saying '2 tsp caraway seeds' used to convert at sauce",
    "density. Nothing re-parses the existing catalog - the specs carry grams already - so no shipped",
    "gram figure moves; only new parses get the better number."
  ],
